xargs did not work at all before this patch, and the cause is in the C
library rather than in xargs.

xargs forks, and the child execs.  It is scrupulous about calling _exit()
rather than exit() everywhere on the child's error paths, precisely so that
the atexit handler the PARENT registered -- wait_for_proc_all -- never runs
in a child; the assertion this patch removes is there to catch a maintainer
who forgets.  The comment above it says as much.

On this target the child cannot obey that rule.  ntlibc has no call that
replaces a running image, so its execve() starts the program as a child,
waits for it and ends with exit(WEXITSTATUS(status)) -- src/process/exec.c
explains the design in its own header comment, and it is the same thing
every from-scratch Unix-on-Windows layer does.  But exit() runs the atexit
handlers, and after fork() those are the parent's.  So in every xargs child,
AFTER the command has already run and printed its output correctly,
wait_for_proc_all runs with getpid() != parent, the assertion fails, and the
child dies of SIGABRT.  The parent then reports

    xargs.exe: /path/to/echo: terminated by signal 6

and exits 125.  Measured on ten different invocations -- default, -n, -L,
-0, -I{} -- all ten identical, and all ten with the command's own output
already correct on stdout above the error.

find is NOT affected, and the difference is instructive: find's launch()
does fflush(stdout)/fflush(stderr) before forking, and its one atexit
handler (close_stdout) is idempotent on an empty buffer, so the same
inherited-handler run is harmless there.  It was checked rather than
assumed: `find . -print -exec echo X {} ';'` redirected to a file -- fully
buffered, where a double flush would show -- produces exactly the host's ten
lines, each once.

The fix here is to return rather than assert.  That is safe rather than
merely quiet: the handler's only job is to reap children and adjust the exit
status; a child has none to reap; and returning lets ntlibc's exit() go on
to the remaining handler and then exit with the status the command really
returned, which is what the parent's waitpid reads.  With the patch, all ten
invocations above match the host's xargs byte for byte, including the empty
input that must not run the command.

This is an ntlibc defect and the real fix belongs there -- an exec emulation
must not run the caller's atexit handlers, so that exit() should be _exit().
The patch is harmless once that is fixed: getpid() will equal parent in the
only process that reaches it.

--- xargs/xargs.c
+++ xargs/xargs.c
@@ -1645,7 +1645,29 @@
    * Instead the child processes should call _exit ().  If someone
    * forgets this rule, you may see the following assert () fail.
    */
-  assert (getpid () == parent);
+  /* ... and on this target the child cannot obey that rule, because it is
+     the C library that breaks it: ntlibc has no call that replaces a
+     running image, so execve() spawns the program, waits for it, and ends
+     with exit(WEXITSTATUS) -- src/process/exec.c says so in its own header
+     comment.  That exit() runs the atexit handlers this process inherited
+     across fork(), and this is one of them.  So the assertion below fires
+     in EVERY xargs child, after the command has already run correctly, and
+     aborts it with SIGABRT; xargs then reports its own child as "terminated
+     by signal 6" and exits 125.  Every invocation, not an edge case.
+
+     Returning instead of asserting is the whole fix, and it is safe rather
+     than merely quiet: this handler's only job is to reap children and
+     adjust the exit status, the child has none to reap, and leaving it
+     early lets ntlibc's exit() carry on to the remaining handler
+     (close_stdin, harmless on a child) and then exit with the status the
+     command really returned -- which is what the parent's waitpid reads.
+
+     This is an ntlibc defect and belongs there: an exec emulation must not
+     run the caller's atexit handlers, so that exit() should be _exit().
+     Reported; the patch stays until the pin moves past it, and is harmless
+     if it does.  */
+  if (getpid () != parent)
+    return;
 
   if (waiting)
     return;
